snippet.textDisplay:
@Mallard942  "You think industry, which still largely operates using machinery and technology decades old, will rush to replace people, a renewable and stable resource, with robots, an exclusive product with copyright and proprietary parts, that will break down and remain broken until you fix or replace it?" yes. Its happening in china rapidly. And now that the US is imposing tariffs on all manner of foreign manufactured goods, its a good bet investment in automation in the US will take off. It already has for major US companies that are being forced to onshore manufacturing. Tbh this tariff war probably accelerated things by about 5 years.

"Machinery is expensive as shit." yes, the custom purpose-built stuff def is. But not mass produced humanoids. Unitree, a chinese company, is already mass producing humanoids (~15k for consumer model), albeit the hands aren't quite as good as this one. But let's see how the new version looks next year.

"Another advantage of employees is that they're a subscription model. If work declines, workers get fired and costs decrease. If robots run out of work, you don't get a rebate, the one-time cost is already paid and you start losing money." Yea but...that employee is making ~50k per year? Plus benefits? Paid vacations? Not to mention all the health and safety costs, insurance, HR admin costs, oof. Idk seems to me a one-time payment of ~20k per a humanoid (bought with bulk discount at let's say 100 units) plus some ongoing maintenance contract with the vendor (let's say $2000 per unit per year), will work out to be much cheaper than human employees, even with periods of slow work. Also, you could probably re-sell the units in the worst case. Given they are general purpose humanoids, it wouldn't be too hard to find buyers.

